WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.320
A tissue is not one average.

2
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.960
Single-cell RNA sequencing reveals a mixture of cell types and changing states.

3
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.600
A type is a stable identity, such as a T cell.

4
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:18.720
A state is variation within it, such as cycling or stress.

5
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.400
In melanoma, malignant cells within one tumour differed in cell-cycle programmes.

6
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.800
That matters because separate clusters can represent different states of one type.

7
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.480
A cluster needs an explanation before it gets a name.

8
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:35.730
Separate the cells.

9
00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.390
Tag each cell's RNA with a shared barcode, then sequence and count.

10
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.010
The matrix has genes as rows and cells as columns.

11
00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:49.250
Group similar cells, then annotate their clusters using known expression profiles.

12
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:52.553
But a cluster can also be an artifact.

13
00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:54.973
Two cells can share a barcode.

14
00:00:56.133 --> 00:00:58.913
Sample handling can separate cells that belong together.

15
00:00:59.813 --> 00:01:02.253
Check annotations with independent evidence.

16
00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:04.953
RNA does not measure protein.

17
00:01:06.013 --> 00:01:09.953
CITE-seq measures surface proteins alongside RNA in the same cell.

18
00:01:10.917 --> 00:01:13.377
An expression path suggests a transition.

19
00:01:14.277 --> 00:01:15.877
It does not track one living cell.

20
00:01:16.857 --> 00:01:18.257
Sequencing destroys each cell.

21
00:01:19.277 --> 00:01:23.517
Inherited barcodes establish ancestry by linking sisters and their descendants.

22
00:01:24.537 --> 00:01:29.317
In mouse blood progenitors, sisters shared fate biases their RNA profiles missed.

23
00:01:30.237 --> 00:01:34.657
Perturbation tests cause: block a candidate, check the outcome, then restore it.

24
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.010
A bulk measurement can change because the mixture changes, even when expression

25
00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.770
within each type stays fixed.

26
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.230
More cells expressing a gene strongly can raise the tissue average.

27
00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.810
Single-cell data separate those possibilities.

28
00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:55.150
But losing fragile cells during dissociation can bias the measured proportions.

29
00:01:56.283 --> 00:01:59.903
Tissue health also depends on balancing cell gain and loss.

30
00:02:00.863 --> 00:02:05.203
In mouse oesophagus, progenitors divide, daughters differentiate, and cells are

31
00:02:05.203 --> 00:02:10.523
shed. In crowded cultured sheets and zebrafish epidermis, living cells can be

32
00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:13.363
squeezed out. The extruded cells then die.

33
00:02:14.323 --> 00:02:16.463
Cell interactions help maintain that balance.

34
00:02:17.417 --> 00:02:19.377
A smaller point closes the story.

35
00:02:20.257 --> 00:02:24.837
In mouse oesophagus, Notch1-mutant clones expanded without dividing faster.

36
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:29.997
Tracking was consistent with more progenitor daughters and fewer differentiating

37
00:02:29.997 --> 00:02:30.257
ones.
